prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 5–10 sentences: the PR is approved and I say "ship it". What will you do to close the feature?
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers eight behaviors, in order. Steps 1–3 are spec / docs / backlog edits *staged together* and committed as one or more `close(<feature>):` commits *before* the squash-merge — this is what keeps `main`'s "one logical commit per feature" invariant. (1) **Reconcile in-flight markers** in the feature spec — unchecked Implementation Plan items, open Decision Log entries, unresolved questions — resolved in place or moved out (backlog or follow-up feature). (2) **Soft project.md verify** — offer to update project.md with what changed; user decline is fine, not a hard block. (3) **Backlog cleanup (interactive)** — list specs/backlog/ and ask the user which items this feature resolves, then `git rm` the chosen ones; *asking is mandatory even when the answer is "none"*. (4) **Commit + push** the staged closure edits as `close(<feature>):` commits. (5) **Closure Review Gate** — body rewritten to final shape (Why + Approach + Reading order grouped by phase + Verification + optional Pairs with / Spec & docs touched). **The PR title is rewritten** from `close(<feature>):` to the dominant agent-dev subject (`feat(<scope>): subject` etc.) so the squash-merge commit on main reads as a feature ship, not a closure mechanic. (6) **Squash merge to main** — one logical commit per feature; the conditional exceptions (single-commit branch, FF-only main, non-linear main) are reference material, not required to be enumerated. (7) **Branch cleanup** — delete the feature branch local + remote without per-step prompts; deletion is the declared default. (8) **Worktree teardown is offer-only** when the working directory is a non-primary worktree; do not auto-remove. Wrong answers: doing any of these without the explicit human cue; skipping the title rewrite at the Closure Review Gate so `main` ends up with `close(<feature>):` as the squash subject; staging closure edits *after* the squash-merge; auto-removing the worktree; treating project.md update as a hard block; detecting resolved backlog items via metadata instead of asking; merging via plain merge by default; leaving the remote branch undeleted.
tools: claude,kiro
